+ * instead of being packed tightly (8 bytes each). This must match the
+ * client-side definition of the same name in dlls/ntdll/unix/esync.c
+ * exactly, since both sides compute byte offsets from the same shm_idx
+ * independently.
+ *
+ * One cache line (64 bytes on essentially every relevant CPU, including all
+ * current ARM cores) avoids false sharing: with the old 8-byte packing, up
+ * to 8 unrelated sync objects could land on the same cache line, so two
+ * threads hammering *different*, unrelated mutexes/events/semaphores would
+ * still bounce the same cache line between cores. It also leaves room for
+ * the mutex's new "waiters" field (see struct mutex below) without shrinking
+ * that margin further. The extra memory cost is one page per ~64 objects
+ * instead of one page per ~512, which is negligible for the handle counts
+ * real applications use. */
